Activity-Relay for Debian and Ubuntu
====================================

The package is intentionally inactive after installation. It generates the
relay identity once, but it does not invent a public hostname, enable a web
server, or start network services.

Initial configuration
---------------------

1. Copy and edit the relay configuration:

   cp /etc/activity-relay/config.yml.example /etc/activity-relay/config.yml
   editor /etc/activity-relay/config.yml
   chown root:activity-relay /etc/activity-relay/config.yml
   chmod 0640 /etc/activity-relay/config.yml

2. The installer creates /etc/activity-relay/actor.pem with mode 0600. Back it
   up before enabling the service. Package upgrades never replace it.

3. The package provides a dedicated Redis instance on 127.0.0.1:6380. It does
   not use or reconfigure the distribution's default Redis service.

4. Enable the native services explicitly:

   systemctl enable --now activity-relay-server.service
   systemctl enable --now activity-relay-worker.service
   systemctl enable --now activity-relay-resource-guard.timer

Starting the API server or worker also starts activity-relay-redis.service.

Package upgrades restart Activity-Relay services that are already active and
print the restart result when configuration finishes. Inactive services are
not enabled or started by an upgrade.

Website and reverse proxy
-------------------------

The website is optional. The relay can operate with a custom page, a redirect,
or no frontend. The reverse proxy must still forward the relay endpoints.
See /usr/share/activity-relay/web/README.md for all frontend modes.


Package-owned website sources are in /usr/share/activity-relay/web. Initialize
an editable copy without replacing existing files:

   cp -an /usr/share/activity-relay/web/. /etc/activity-relay-web/

Edit /etc/activity-relay-web/site.json and build the site:

   /etc/activity-relay-web/rebuild-site.sh

The optional activitypub_contact and activitypub_contact_url settings add a
public ActivityPub operator contact to the generated footer. They do not affect
relay processing. Rerun rebuild-site.sh whenever website configuration or
source content changes.

Nginx and Apache examples are installed under:

   /usr/share/doc/activity-relay/examples/nginx/
   /usr/share/doc/activity-relay/examples/apache/

Neither example is installed into sites-available, enabled, or reloaded by the
package. Both expect /var/www/activity-relay/public as the document root.

The Nginx example includes commented HTTP/3/QUIC directives. Enable them only
when nginx -V reports --with-http_v3_module and UDP port 443 is open. Keep the
TCP 443 listener enabled for HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 fallback.

Mail
----

Resource alerts default to /usr/bin/mail from mailutils. Configure an MTA and
root forwarding, or set ADMIN_EMAIL. A sendmail-compatible backend can instead
be selected in config.yml.

Scheduled summaries use server-local `HH:MM` values:

   DAILY_SUMMARY_EMAIL: true
   DAILY_SUMMARY_TIMES:
     - "08:00"
     - "14:30"

The five-minute timer processes each slot on its first run at or after the
configured time, so delivery may be several minutes late. Each slot sends at
most once per local calendar day. Changing or adding a time creates another
eligible slot that day.

After downtime, only the most recent due slot is sent. The catch-up email lists
earlier skipped slots. View the persistent state and archived sent reports at:

   /var/lib/activity-relay-guard/summary-slots.json
   /var/lib/activity-relay-guard/summaries/YYYY-MM-DD/

Skipped slots have no historical report body because no snapshot existed at
that time. Administrative commands are:

   activity-relay-resource-guard --show-summary-state
   activity-relay-resource-guard --preview-summary
   activity-relay-resource-guard --send-summary-now
   activity-relay-resource-guard --reset-summary-slot "14:30" --force
   activity-relay-resource-guard --reset-summary-state --force

A reset slot produces a current report when the guard runs again; it cannot
reconstruct the historical state. `--send-summary-now` does not consume a
scheduled slot. `--preview-summary` and `--no-mail` do not record a summary
slot.

`DAILY_SUMMARY_HOUR` remains as a deprecated single-slot `HH:00` setting.
`DAILY_SUMMARY_TIMES` takes precedence. Confirm the server timezone with
`timedatectl`.

`MAIL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` limits the local mail command. The systemd unit also has
a two-minute oneshot timeout and permits common local-MTA spool paths. A custom
MTA that writes elsewhere may require a local systemd drop-in.


Storage placement
-----------------

The single-disk default stores Redis at /var/lib/activity-relay/redis and cache
data at /var/cache/activity-relay. To move Redis to another filesystem:

* stop all Activity-Relay services;
* move the data while preserving redis:redis ownership;
* update dir in /etc/activity-relay/redis.conf;
* update STORAGE_DIR, STORAGE_LIMIT, STORAGE_REQUIRE_MOUNT, and
  STORAGE_MOUNT_POINT in /etc/activity-relay/config.yml;
* ensure the filesystem is mounted before activity-relay-redis.service starts.

Removal
-------

Package removal and purge retain actor.pem, config.yml, editable website files,
and Redis data. This prevents package operations from destroying relay identity
or state. Delete them manually only after testing backups.


Upgrade-safe website customization
----------------------------------

Use the current package-managed builder:

   activity-relay-rebuild-site

Do not rebuild from an old copied builder under /etc/activity-relay-web.
The command reads current source from /usr/share/activity-relay/web, website
settings from /etc/activity-relay-web/site.json, relay branding/operator
metadata from /etc/activity-relay/config.yml, and operator HTML overrides from
/etc/activity-relay-web/content.

Runtime and shared public metadata:

   /etc/activity-relay/config.yml

Website identity and contact settings:

   /etc/activity-relay-web/site.json

Optional content overrides:

   /etc/activity-relay-web/content/home.html
   /etc/activity-relay-web/content/about.html
   /etc/activity-relay-web/content/rules.html
   /etc/activity-relay-web/content/privacy.html
   /etc/activity-relay-web/content/footer.html

Copy only files that need customization from
/usr/share/activity-relay/web/content. Generated files under
/var/www/activity-relay/public are replaced on every rebuild.
